N2  - Introduction: This study aimed to explore the effect of transcutaneous electrical acupoint stimulation (TEAS) on postoperative delirium (POD) in older patients undergoing laparoscopic gastrointestinal surgery, and preliminarily analyzed the potential role of tryptophan-kynurenine pathway. Methods: A total of 106 older patients undergoing laparoscopic gastrointestinal surgery were randomized to the TEAS and control groups. In the TEAS group, the bilateral Hegu, Neiguan, Quchi and Zusanli received electric stimulation 30 min before anesthesia induction until the end of surgery, while the same acupoints were selected but no current in the control group. The POD and subtypes were assessed according to Confusion Assessment Method and Richmond Agitation and Sedation Scale within postoperative 7 days. We measured the interleukin-1β (IL-1β) and S100 calcium-binding protein β (S100β) levels. Tryptophan (TRY) and kynurenine (KYN) levels were quantitated by liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) analysis, then KYN/TRY ratio was calculated to reflect the activity of the indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO). Any intervention-related adverse events were recorded, and participants were required to discontinue the trial and receive appropriate treatment immediately. Results: The incidence of POD was significantly lower in the TEAS group (11.5% VS 27.6%, P = 0.036). Simultaneously, IL-1β and S100β levels of TEAS group patients were decreased immediately and the first day after surgery (P < 0.05). Furthermore, the TEAS group exhibited a significant increase in plasma tryptophan levels and a decrease in kynurenine as well as KYN/TRY ratio (P < 0.05). Also, a positive correlation between KYN/TRY ratio with IL-1β and S100β was shown. No adverse events occurred in this study. Conclusion: TEAS could improve POD in older patients undergoing laparoscopic gastrointestinal surgery, which might be relevant to downregulating the tryptophan-kynurenine pathway and inhibiting inflammatory response. Clinical trial registration: ChiCTR2400083164
