N2  - Purpose (the aim of the study): This study aimed to comprehensively assess minimally invasive non-surgical interventions for plantar fasciitis, comparing their effectiveness in pain relief across short-, medium-, and long-term durations, with the ultimate goal of proposing an optimal treatment approach for plantar fasciitis. Methods: Electronic databases including MEDLINE, EMBASE, Cochrane Central, PEDro, CNKI, CiNii, KMBASE and KISS were searched up to June 2021. Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing eight minimally invasive nonsurgical treatments (acupuncture, electroacupuncture, acupotomy, corticosteroid injection, dextrose prolotherapy, botulinum toxin type A (BTX-A) injection, platelet rich plasma injection, autologous whole blood injection), extracorporeal shockwave therapy, ultrasound therapy, low-level laser therapy, oral nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and placebo in adult patients with plantar fasciitis were included. Two independent reviewers selected studies, extracted data, and assessed risk of bias. The main outcome was the degree of pain relief, which was evaluated by dividing it into short-term, medium-term, and long-term. A network meta-analysis was performed using the Bayesian method and a random effect model was applied. The ranking of interventions was obtained through the rank probabilities and the surface under the cumulative rank (SUCRA). Consistency test, publication bias test, and sensitivity analysis were performed to evaluate the reliability of the results. Results: Our analysis encompassed 78 studies involving 5,623 patients. In the short-term, BTX-A injection exhibited the highest efficacy compared to placebo (Standard Mean Difference (SMD): -2.9, 95% Credible Interval (CrI): -4.3 to -1.5), and this trend continued into the medium-term (SMD: -3.3, 95% CrI: -4.4 to -2.0). Acupotomy emerged as the most effective treatment in the long-term (SMD: -4.0, 95% CrI: -6.8 to -1.2). According to the Surface Under the Cumulative Rank (SUCRA) values, BTX-A injection and acupotomy were consistently identified as the most promising options for pain relief. Adverse events were reported in 73% of studies, with most being mild and transient. While consistency was affirmed across all periods, publication bias was detected. A sensitivity analysis of relevant studies confirmed the robustness of the ranking results. Conclusions: Our findings provide valuable clinical insights by identifying more effective treatments for alleviating pain in plantar fasciitis, mainly focusing on eight widely utilized minimally invasive nonsurgical treatments. BTX-A injection and acupotomy emerged as the top recommendations for pain relief in plantar fasciitis. However, cautious interpretation is warranted due to confirmed heterogeneity and publication bias. Consequently, the need for high-quality, large-scale RCTs for each intervention is emphasized. [Formula presented] Network plot The size of the nodes is proportional to the number of studies, and the thickness of the edge is proportional to the number of studies comparing the two interventions directly. Blue text indicates the number of direct comparison studies and is only shown if there are more than four. [Formula presented] The cumulative ranking curves of each treatment for short-term outcomes. [Formula presented] The cumulative ranking curves of each treatment for medium-term outcomes. [Formula presented] The cumulative ranking curves of each treatment for long-term outcomes.
