N2  - Background: In a prior three-arm, multicenter, randomized controlled trial, it was discovered that lectro-acupuncture (EA) was associated with promising effect in improving the primary endpoint of quality of life and symptom burden in gastric cancer patients undergoing adjuvant chemotherapy. The findings also indicated that EA was associated with with a lower incidence of grade 3/4 myelosuppression. This study aims to initially explore its underlying mechanisms. Method: Thirty-nine patients were recruited from a randomized controlled study that investigated EA to improve the quality of life of patients with adjuvant chemotherapy for gastric cancer. Twelve patients received high-dose EA (HA group, seven times each chemotherapy cycle for three cycles), thirteen patients received low-dose EA (LA group, three times each chemotherapy cycle), and fourteen patients only received usual care. At the baseline and the end of the third chemotherapy cycle, the peripheral venous blood of patients was collected to measure the concentration of chemokines. Results: In the control group, the degree of platelet decline was positively correlated with the concentration of interleukin- 8(IL-8) (r=0.540, p=0.046), while the degree of anemia was negatively connected with the concentration of macrophage inflammatory protein 1α(MIP-1α) (r=-0.578, p=0.030). In the LA group, the degree of leukocyte decline was negatively correlated with the concentration change of IL-8 (r=-0.626, p=0.022), while in the HA group, the degree of neutrophil decline was positively correlated with the concentration change of MIP-1b (r=0.582, p=0.047). Conclusion: In gastric cancer patients undergoing adjuvant chemotherapy, the reduction of grade 3/4 myelosuppression by EA appears to be associated with the regulation of changes in chemokine concentrations, but further studies are still needed to clarify it.
